I am closing this for now, as the source of the error is likely at my end (I have a hard time believing that the `V8_DEPRECATED` or `V8_DEPRECATE_SOON` macro situated before the `V8_EXPORT` macro would cause C++ compiler issues).

Here is the diff from 41 to 44:

`41`:
```
  class V8_EXPORT Value {
   public:
    V8_DEPRECATE_SOON(
        "Prefer using String::ValueView if you can, or string->Write to a "
        "buffer if you cannot.")
    Value(Isolate* isolate, Local<v8::Value> obj);
```

`42`:
```
  class V8_DEPRECATE_SOON(
      "Prefer using String::ValueView if you can, or string->Write to a "
      "buffer if you cannot.") V8_EXPORT Value {
   public:
    V8_DEPRECATED(
        "Prefer using String::ValueView if you can, or string->Write to a "
        "buffer if you cannot.")
    Value(Isolate* isolate, Local<v8::Value> obj);
```

`43`:
```
  class V8_DEPRECATED(
      "Prefer using String::ValueView if you can, or string->Write to a "
      "buffer if you cannot.") V8_EXPORT Value {
   public:
    V8_DEPRECATED(
        "Prefer using String::ValueView if you can, or string->Write to a "
        "buffer if you cannot.")
    Value(Isolate* isolate, Local<v8::Value> obj);
```

`44`:
```
  class V8_DEPRECATED(
      "Prefer using String::ValueView if you can, or string->Write to a "
      "buffer if you cannot.") V8_EXPORT Value {
   public:
    V8_DEPRECATED(
        "Prefer using String::ValueView if you can, or string->Write to a "
        "buffer if you cannot.")
    Value(Isolate* isolate, Local<v8::Value> obj);
```

Note that in NodeJS `26.8.1`, `class V8_EXPORT Value` is neither `V8_DEPRECATE_SOON` or `V8_DEPRECATED`, so I wonder why the Electron headers for Electron `42`, `43` and `44` (NodeJS `24`) applies the deprecation on the class? (also, isn't the macro located in the wrong slot?)

https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/7be6d3af31a65adea57c94c41e50c2b071ed0b3a/deps/v8/include/v8-primitive.h#L622-L627

```
  class V8_EXPORT Value {
   public:
    V8_DEPRECATE_SOON(
        "Prefer using String::ValueView if you can, or string->Write to a "
        "buffer if you cannot.")
    Value(Isolate* isolate, Local<v8::Value> obj);
```

